    <details class="bcv2-section" id="about" open>
      <summary>
        <span class="bcv2-section-title">About The Negotiator II</span>
      </summary>

      <ul class="bcv2-specs" aria-label="At a glance">
        <li>USB PD 3.1</li>
        <li>5 to 48V in 0.1V steps</li>
        <li>Up to 5A</li>
        <li>2 mm thick total</li>
        <li>190 LEDs</li>
        <li>$8 a board</li>
        <li>$3.8 BOM cost</li>
      </ul>

      <p class="bcv2-lead">The Negotiator II is here! USB PD 3.1: Unlocked.</p>
      <p>USB PD (Power Delivery) is a miracle, behind which is a lot of complexity. USB Chargers today have chips much more powerful than the Apollo 11 moon landing guidance computer, so we cant just grab a charger and get 15V out of it, all that capability is locked away, and the Negotiator is the key.</p>

      <p>With the Negotiator II, you can find out exactly what any USB port can do with the capability bar and mode selector. Then, select whatever voltage your charger supports between 5 to 48V in 0.1V increments and you’ll get that. Selecting a voltage will show you what current your charger can provide at that voltage, this card can deliver up to 5A! Apply your selection to initiate the negotiation and you’ll see a live measurement of the voltage, current, and power at the card’s input.</p>
      <figure class="bcv2-figure">
        <img src="{{ img }}capabilities-700.webp" srcset="{{ img }}capabilities-700.webp 700w, {{ img }}capabilities-1400.webp 1400w" sizes="(max-width: 860px) calc(100vw - 40px), 820px" width="1400" height="1050" alt="The capability bar lit from 5 to 48V, with the PPS CV and CL lights on" loading="lazy" decoding="async">
        <figcaption>An EPR charger lights up the whole bar, 5 to 48V.</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <p>Use it to power your load from any USB C charger/powerbank when out on the field. Connect your load using one of the various connector-less solutions shown in the photo below. Im pretty proud of the wire wedge! Its a low profile, quick, and cheap wire-to-board connector. Wedge the wire in, pull it parallel to the board as shown, done! Otherwise, use banana plug, a crocodile clip, or friction fit 0.1” header. At 2 mm thick including the PCB and components, It’s small enough to go into your bag or wallet so you always have a power supply handy.</p>
      <figure class="bcv2-figure bcv2-figure-clear">
        <img src="{{ img }}connectors-v3-700.webp" srcset="{{ img }}connectors-v3-700.webp 700w, {{ img }}connectors-v3-1400.webp 1400w" sizes="(max-width: 860px) calc(100vw - 40px), 820px" width="1400" height="1038" alt="Header pins pressed into the 0.1 inch holes, banana plugs in the wire wedge holes, and a crocodile clip and a wedged wire on the wire wedge" loading="lazy" decoding="async">
        <figcaption>0.1” header, banana plugs, and the wire wedge with a crocodile clip and a wire.</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <p>This was a challenging and rewarding board to design. I managed to get the cost down to $8 per board delivered for a quantity of 100. When you consider all the capabilities, 0.8mm 4-layer ENIG plated PCB, the sheer number of components (including about 200 LEDs!), I think it’s certainly an achievement to be proud of.</p>

      <p>Powering around 200 LEDs from 48V was fun, especially trying to do it as cheaply as possible and with components under 1.2 mm thick. I could not afford to use a buck regulator or expensive ultra efficient LEDs. These 0402 LEDs produce 10 mcd (bright enough for outdoors) with less than 0.2 mA and cost $0.004 each. The LDO works well with firmware throttling, feeling warm and not uncomfortable to touch.</p>


      <p>With the VBUS and CC pins next to each other, a VBUS to CC short at 48V is a real and dangerous possibility. TI’s TPD4S480 is the right chip for the job, However, It costs $1.1 @1k qty. Instead I used depletion mode N-channel mosfets which deplete, cutting off CC and protecting the MCU. I tested shorting 48V to CC input – no current or heat, and negotiation worked afterwards.</p>

      <p>This card shows the USB PD modes, in the photo below, you can see the different modes that this card supports. Auto combines all modes and selects the highest current available at every voltage. PDO cannot do 9.2V, you’ll need PPS or AVS for that. Multiple modes can support the same voltage at different current limits.</p>
      <figure class="bcv2-figure bcv2-figure-tall">
        <img src="{{ img }}mode-selections-420.webp" srcset="{{ img }}mode-selections-420.webp 420w, {{ img }}mode-selections-840.webp 840w" sizes="(max-width: 460px) calc(100vw - 40px), 420px" width="840" height="1490" alt="The PD mode lights and the capability bar in AUTO, PDO and PPS on the same charger, each lighting a different set of voltages" loading="lazy" decoding="async">
        <figcaption>AUTO, PDO and PPS on the same charger.</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <p>To make this card a reality, I had to write a library for it. I plan on open sourcing the PD version of that and releasing <em>the</em> cheapest PD 3.1 Sink design, ever. So check back here soon!</p>

