    <details class="bcv2-section" id="instructions">
      <summary>
        <span class="bcv2-section-title">Instructions</span>
        <span class="bcv2-section-dek">Got one of my cards? Heres how to use it.</span>
      </summary>

      <p>So you got one of my business cards, awesome! Its also a tiny USB PD power supply, here's how to use it.</p>
      <p>Want to see everything its doing? Plug it into your computer and open the <a href="/bcv2/console/">console</a>.</p>
      <p><em>This page is a work in progress, I will add more information and polish it... Eventually.</em></p>

      <details class="bcv2-sub" id="serial">
        <summary>
          <span class="bcv2-sub-title">Control it from your own code</span>
          <span class="bcv2-sub-dek">The USB protocol the console uses, and a Python script</span>
        </summary>

        <p>The card speaks a small binary protocol over USB serial which the console uses. Here's how to make your own script to talk to it. To get going quickly, grab <a href="/bcv2/bcv2.py" download>bcv2.py</a>, a Python script that handles the protocol.</p>

        <h4>Connect</h4>
        <p>Plug {{ silk("DATA") }} into your computer and it shows up as a serial port: a COM port on Windows, /dev/ttyACM0 on Linux, /dev/cu.usbmodem-something on a Mac. Its USB ID is 1A86:FE0C. The baud rate doesn't matter since it's USB. Set DTR when you open the port to get the communication going. pyserial and most serial terminals set it for you.</p>
        <p>Once the port is open, the card sends its device info, then a reading every 50 ms. When its USB queue is full, the card drops readings, device info and charger offers instead of waiting, so ask again if one doesn't come (the console asks for device info up to three times, 400 ms apart). Answers to your commands aren't dropped.</p>
        <p>Firmware v1.2 and earlier can also lose the first packet each way after the port opens, every other time it opens. Start with a device query (0x01) and send it again if no answer comes within half a second, like the console does. Once it's answered, later commands get through.</p>

        <h4>Frames</h4>
        <p>Everything, both ways, is a frame like this. Numbers are little-endian.</p>
        <div class="bcv2-table">
          <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Byte</th><th>What</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td>0, 1</td><td>0x50 0x44, "PD"</td></tr>
              <tr><td>2</td><td>Version, 1</td></tr>
              <tr><td>3</td><td>Kind: which command or event</td></tr>
              <tr><td>4</td><td>Sequence number</td></tr>
              <tr><td>5</td><td>Payload length, 0 to 56</td></tr>
              <tr><td>6 on</td><td>Payload</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Last</td><td>CRC-8 of byte 2 to the end of the payload: polynomial 0x07, starting at 0, no final XOR</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p>Give each command a sequence number from 1 to 255. The card answers each one with a result (kind 0x80) with the same sequence number, and the events it sends on its own have 0. To read frames, find 0x50 0x44, wait for the whole frame and check the CRC. If it doesn't match, skip one byte and look for the next 0x50 0x44.</p>
        <pre><code>def crc8(data):
    crc = 0
    for byte in data:
        crc ^= byte
        for _ in range(8):
            crc = ((crc &lt;&lt; 1) ^ 0x07) &amp; 0xFF if crc &amp; 0x80 else (crc &lt;&lt; 1) &amp; 0xFF
    return crc

def frame(kind, seq, payload=b""):
    body = bytes([1, kind, seq, len(payload)]) + payload
    return b"PD" + body + bytes([crc8(body)])

frame(0x01, 1)  # ask for device info: 50 44 01 01 01 00 68</code></pre>

        <h4>Commands</h4>
        <div class="bcv2-table">
          <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Kind</th><th>Payload</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td>0x01</td><td>None</td><td>Sends device info (0x81).</td></tr>
              <tr><td>0x02</td><td>None</td><td>Asks the charger for its offers again (0x83).</td></tr>
              <tr><td>0x10</td><td>9 bytes: voltage in mV (u32), current in mA (u32, 0xFFFFFFFF for the most on offer), supply (u8: 0 auto, 1 fixed, 2 PPS, 3 SPR AVS, 4 EPR AVS)</td><td>Requests a voltage, like {{ key("APPLY") }}. 5000 mV or more, and 500 mA or more.</td></tr>
              <tr><td>0x0C</td><td>None</td><td>Turns the output on, like {{ key("LOAD ON/OFF") }}. The same safety rules apply.</td></tr>
              <tr><td>0x0D</td><td>None</td><td>Turns the output off.</td></tr>
              <tr><td>0x05</td><td>None</td><td>Enters EPR, for 22V and up.</td></tr>
              <tr><td>0x07</td><td>None</td><td>Exits EPR.</td></tr>
              <tr><td>0x06</td><td>None</td><td>Asks the charger for its EPR offers again (0x83).</td></tr>
              <tr><td>0x32</td><td>None</td><td>Reads the black box (0xA7).</td></tr>
              <tr><td>0x34</td><td>3 bytes: brightness 0 to 43, dim dots 3 to 60, flags (1 hides leading zeros, 2 hides the marks on the bar)</td><td>Sets the display, and the card keeps it. The defaults are 34, 21 and 0. From firmware v1.1.</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p>The result (0x80) is one byte: 0 queued, 1 busy, 2 invalid, 3 unsupported. Busy means the card already holds 4 commands, so wait a moment and send it again. Queued means the card took the command, not that the charger agreed, so watch the readings for the contract to change. It changes to your request rounded down to the charger's step (20 mV for PPS, 100 mV for AVS). If it doesn't change, the charger said no and {{ silk("WARNING") }} plays R.</p>

        <h4>Events</h4>
        <div class="bcv2-table">
          <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Kind</th><th>Bytes</th><th>What</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td>0x80</td><td>1</td><td>Command result, above.</td></tr>
              <tr><td>0xA5</td><td>22</td><td>A reading, every 50 ms. Card uptime in ms (0 to 3), VBUS in mV (4 to 7), current in mA (8 to 11), contract mV (12 to 15, 0 when there's none), contract mA (16 to 19), flags (20 and 21, below).</td></tr>
              <tr><td>0x81</td><td>32</td><td>Device info. Card ID (0 to 7), flags (8: bit 0 PPS, bit 1 EPR), max mV (9 to 12), max mA (13 to 16), max mW (17 to 20), firmware build (21 to 24, 0x0105004F is v1.2), library version (25 to 28), display settings as in 0x34 (29 to 31). Firmware before v1.1 sends 21 or 29 bytes.</td></tr>
              <tr><td>0x83</td><td>3, plus 4 per offer</td><td>Charger offers. 0 for an SPR list or 1 for EPR (0), the charger can do EPR (1), how many offers (2), then each offer as a 4-byte PDO from byte 3. Sent when a charger is plugged in, after 0x02 or 0x06, and when EPR starts.</td></tr>
              <tr><td>0x88</td><td>6</td><td>Hard reset. 0 if the charger sent it, 1 if the card did (0), recovery time in ms (1 to 4), cause (5). The output is off after one.</td></tr>
              <tr><td>0xA7</td><td>56</td><td>Black box. Saved supply as in 0x10 (3), saved mV (8 to 11) and mA (12 to 15), how many events (2), then up to 6 events of 6 bytes from byte 20: uptime in seconds (u16), context (u16), kind (u8: 1 hard reset, 2 PHY reset failed, 3 PD PHY unstable, 4 partner timeout, 5 protocol error, 6 terminal stop, 7 EPR entry failed, 8 power rail fell) and detail (u8). The script decodes the rest.</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>

        <h4>Reading flags</h4>
        <div class="bcv2-table">
          <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Bit</th><th>Means</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td>0</td><td>A charger is attached.</td></tr>
              <tr><td>1</td><td>There's a contract.</td></tr>
              <tr><td>2</td><td>LOAD is on, what you asked for.</td></tr>
              <tr><td>3</td><td>The output is actually on.</td></tr>
              <tr><td>4</td><td>The inrush routine started the output, WARN I. From firmware v1.2.</td></tr>
              <tr><td>5</td><td>The display is dimmed for the LDO, {{ silk("THERM LIM") }}.</td></tr>
              <tr><td>11</td><td>The brake tripped. Turn LOAD on again to clear it.</td></tr>
              <tr><td>12 to 14</td><td>What last turned LOAD off: 1 the LOAD key, 2 APPLY, 3 a USB command, 4 the brake, 5 the charger session changed. From firmware v1.2.</td></tr>
              <tr><td>15</td><td>Your settings couldn't be saved, WARN S.</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p>The other bits are for debugging.</p>

        <h4>Offers</h4>
        <p>Each offer is a 32-bit PDO, laid out as the USB PD spec has it. Offers 1 to 7 are SPR and 8 and up are EPR.</p>
        <div class="bcv2-table">
          <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Type, top bits</th><th>Voltage</th><th>Current or power</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td>Fixed, 00</td><td>Bits 19 to 10 × 50 mV</td><td>Bits 9 to 0 × 10 mA</td></tr>
              <tr><td>PPS, 1100</td><td>Bits 15 to 8 × 100 mV, to bits 24 to 17 × 100 mV</td><td>Bits 6 to 0 × 50 mA</td></tr>
              <tr><td>EPR AVS, 1101</td><td>Bits 15 to 8 × 100 mV, to bits 25 to 17 × 100 mV</td><td>Bits 7 to 0, in W</td></tr>
              <tr><td>SPR AVS, 1110</td><td>9V to 15V, or to 20V when it has a 20V current</td><td>Bits 19 to 10 × 10 mA up to 15V, bits 9 to 0 × 10 mA above</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>

        <h4>What the console does</h4>
        <p>When it connects, it sends 0x01 until device info arrives (up to three times, 400 ms apart), sends 0x02 for the offers and starts drawing the readings. A request is one 0x10. A ramp sends one 0x10 at a time, and the next once the contract has changed and VBUS is within an eighth plus 0.5V of it, the card's own check. Tare, averaging and the CSV all happen in the browser.</p>

        <h4>The Python script</h4>
        <p><a href="/bcv2/bcv2.py" download>bcv2.py</a> needs Python 3.8 or later and pyserial (<code>pip install pyserial</code>), and finds the card on its own.</p>
        <pre><code>python bcv2.py                      # card info, charger offers, then live readings
python bcv2.py watch --csv log.csv  # the same, saving every reading like the console's CSV
python bcv2.py request 9            # 9V, the card picks the supply
python bcv2.py request 12.34 2.5 --supply pps
python bcv2.py load on
python bcv2.py epr on
python bcv2.py blackbox</code></pre>
        <p>Or use it from your own script:</p>
        <pre><code>from bcv2 import Card

with Card() as card:
    card.request(15)
    card.load(True)
    for reading in card.readings(seconds=10):
        print(reading["mv"], reading["ma"])</code></pre>
      </details>

      <h3>What can it do?</h3>
      <h4>See what your charger offers</h4>
      <p>Plug it into a USB-C charger and the {{ silk("SOURCE CAPABILITY") }} bar lights up every voltage it can give you, from 5 to 48V. The mode lights show which PD modes it supports (PDO, PPS, SAVS and EAVS), the one its using is brighter. In a few seconds you know if that charger is any good.</p>
      <h4>Power your projects</h4>
      <p>Pick a voltage, press {{ key("APPLY") }} and the card negotiates it with the charger. Then press {{ key("LOAD ON/OFF") }} to send it to your load. A very slim and portable power supply, all you need is a PD charger and a cable!</p>

      <h3>How to use it</h3>
      <div class="bcv2-steps">
        <h4>Plug it in</h4>
        <p>Plug a USB PD charger into the {{ silk("POWER") }} port. The displays show the voltage, current and power going out, and the bar shows what the charger offers.</p>

        <h4>Hook up your load</h4>
        <p>Use the {{ silk("WIRE WEDGE") }}, banana plugs, or the 0.1" headers. Wedge the wire in like below, or just clip a crocodile clip on it. Keep your load's capacitance under 90uF, its printed on the card too.</p>
        <figure class="bcv2-figure">
          <img src="{{ img }}wire-wedge-700.webp" srcset="{{ img }}wire-wedge-700.webp 700w, {{ img }}wire-wedge-1400.webp 1400w" sizes="(max-width: 860px) calc(100vw - 40px), 820px" width="1400" height="1019" alt="A crocodile clip on the top bar of the wire wedge and a wire wedged under the bottom bar, pulled along the board" loading="lazy" decoding="async">
          <figcaption>A crocodile clip on top, a wedged wire on the bottom.</figcaption>
        </figure>

        <h4>Change the voltage or current</h4>
        <p>Press {{ key("V/A") }} to choose voltage or current, then {{ key("FINE +") }} and {{ key("FINE −") }} for small steps or {{ key("COARSE +") }} and {{ key("COARSE −") }} for big ones. The new value blinks until you press {{ key("APPLY") }}.</p>
        <p>Dont want to press {{ key("APPLY") }} every time? hold it for {{ silk("AUTO APPLY") }}, now every change goes through right away. Hold it again to turn it off.</p>

        <h4>Pick a mode</h4>
        <p>{{ key("MODE") }} picks between AUTO, PDO, PPS, SAVS and EAVS. Tap it and the one you're on blinks, tap again to move to the next one, then press {{ key("APPLY") }} (or just wait 5 seconds). AUTO stays lit dimly while its on.</p>
        <p>If the new mode cant do your voltage, the card goes to the next one down. If it has nothing lower, it goes up to the lowest one it has: your old voltage flashes fast as a warning, and the load turns off before it changes. Want to go up with the load still on? Pick the higher voltage in AUTO.</p>
        <p>Hold {{ key("MODE") }} to turn EPR on or off, that's where 28V and up live.</p>

        <h4>Turn the output on</h4>
        <p>{{ key("LOAD ON/OFF") }} turns your output on and off, and {{ silk("LOAD EN") }} lights up when its on. If {{ silk("LOAD EN") }} blinks, the output cant turn on yet, or the change you're making will turn it off when you apply it.</p>

        <h4>Lock the buttons</h4>
        <p>Hold {{ key("V/A") }} for a second so nothing changes accidentally, and hold it again to unlock. {{ key("LOAD ON/OFF") }} still works while its locked.</p>

        <h4>Change the brightness</h4>
        <p>Hold {{ key("COARSE −") }} and {{ key("MODE") }} for 5 seconds. {{ key("FINE +") }} {{ key("FINE −") }} {{ key("COARSE +") }} {{ key("COARSE −") }} change it and {{ key("APPLY") }} saves it. The slow blinking dot is the default. Needs firmware v1.1, you can also set it from the console.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="bcv2-panel">
        <h3>What the lights mean</h3>
        <p>{{ silk("THERM LIM") }} lights up, {{ silk("TEMP WARN") }} blinks, and the card dims its display to keep LDO thermal dissipation in check starting above 21V on the input. The LDO gets warm to the touch but not uncomfortable, still, it is better not to touch it when that light is blinking.</p>
        <p>{{ silk("PPS CV") }} and {{ silk("PPS CL") }} glow dimly whenever you're on PPS. The bright one shows if the charger is holding your voltage (CV) or limiting your current (CL). Some chargers dont report this correctly, and some only do CL at certain currents.</p>
        <p>If {{ silk("WARNING") }} blinks, its spelling a letter in Morse code. If theres more than one, they take turns:</p>
        <ul class="bcv2-morse">
          <li><strong>R</strong>{{ morse(".-.") }}<span>the charger said no, or it said yes but never gave the voltage (the card tries a few times first).</span></li>
          <li><strong>S</strong>{{ morse("...") }}<span>your settings couldnt be saved.</span></li>
          <li><strong>T</strong>{{ morse("-") }}<span>the power dipped. If that turned your load off, it keeps going until you turn it back on.</span></li>
          <li><strong>F</strong>{{ morse("..-.") }}<span>the card lost power a few times while restoring your last setting, so it started with a safer one. Press {{ key("APPLY") }} to try again.</span></li>
          <li><strong>I</strong>{{ morse("..") }}<span>Due to Inrush, the output didnt turn on (big capacitors and loads might cause this). The card dropped the voltage to aid the charge pump, then raised it back up. Added in firmware v1.2.</span></li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <h3>Chargers are all different</h3>
      <p>Not every charger offers every mode, and some just dont play nice with it. If something doesnt work or acts weird, tell you what, try another cable first - then another charger.</p>
      <div class="bcv2-tips">
        <div class="bcv2-tip">
          <h4>Only seeing 5V?</h4>
          <p>Then you arent using a full PD power supply, so thats all the card can get. Use a USB PD charger or powerbank thats atleast 20W, laptop chargers are usually the best ones.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="bcv2-tip">
          <h4>Want 22V and up?</h4>
          <p>You'll need an EPR charger (these are usually 140W per port or more) and a 240W rated USB-C cable.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h3>The console</h3>
      <h4>Connect</h4>
      <p>The card has a second USB port, {{ silk("DATA") }}, and it only carries data. Keep your charger in {{ silk("POWER") }}, plug {{ silk("DATA") }} into your computer, then open <a href="/bcv2/console/">anasmalas.com/bcv2/console</a> in Chrome or Edge and press Connect. It works in Chrome on Android too.</p>
      <h4>What it does</h4>
      <p>You can see everything your charger offers in detail, request exact voltages, and watch live graphs of voltage, current and power. It also tells you if theres newer firmware for your card and how to update it.</p>
      <p>If your card restarts on its own, the console reads its black box when it reconnects. Thats a small log of what went wrong.</p>
      <h4>Bug reports</h4>
      <p>Bug reports are on by default. When something goes wrong, like the card restarting, the charger saying yes but not giving you the voltage, or the console itself breaking, I get the black box and the log so I can look into it and fix it in the next firmware. It really helps!</p>
      <p>Dont want that? Hit "Turn off" on the little notice at the top, or untick "Share anonymous bug reports" any time.</p>
      <p>You can also press "Report a bug" any time and tell me what happened. Or save the report and send it to the email on the card, scan the QR code on the card to add me to your phone contacts quickly :) Emailing it isnt anonymous of course, but the report still has your card's ID removed.</p>

      <h3 id="update">Updating the firmware</h3>
      <p class="bcv2-callout">Head to <a href="/bcv2/update/">anasmalas.com/bcv2/update</a>, it shows you the easiest way for whatever you're on. On a Mac or an Android phone it updates right in the browser, nothing to install. The console tells you when theres a new one.</p>
      <p>To get an email for new firmware, watch the <a href="{{ bcv2Releases.repo }}" rel="noopener">BCv2 releases</a> on GitHub (Watch &gt; Custom &gt; Releases), or use the <a href="{{ bcv2Releases.feed }}">feed</a>. <a href="/bcv2/changelog/">Whats new</a>.</p>

      <h3 id="privacy">Bug reports and your data</h3>
      <h4>What gets sent</h4>
      <p>Only when something goes wrong, or when you send a report yourself. Turn bug reports off and nothing gets sent unless you hit Send.</p>
      <p>A report has the firmware version, your browser and system (like Chrome 140 on Windows), what your charger offers, the last minute of voltage and current readings, the console log, the black box, any errors from the console itself, and whatever you wrote.</p>
      <p>Reports go to this site (anasmalas.com, which runs on Cloudflare) and get deleted after 180 days.</p>
      <h4>What doesnt</h4>
      <p>Your card's ID is removed before anything is sent, and your IP address isnt stored. No cookies either, your browser just remembers if you turned reports off. Please note that I'll use an LLM to process this data, so please dont write anything youre not willing to share with it. The only other thing the console asks this site for is the latest firmware version.</p>
    </details>

    <section class="bcv2-updates" id="updates" aria-labelledby="updates-title">
      <h2 id="updates-title">Firmware updates</h2>
      <div class="bcv2-updates-links">
        <a class="bcv2-updates-link" href="/bcv2/update/"><span class="bcv2-updates-name">Update your card</span><span class="bcv2-updates-line">Takes a couple of minutes</span></a>
        <a class="bcv2-updates-link" href="/bcv2/changelog/"><span class="bcv2-updates-name">Whats new</span><span class="bcv2-updates-line">Release notes</span></a>
      </div>
      <p class="bcv2-updates-follow">To get an email for new firmware, watch the <a href="{{ bcv2Releases.repo }}" rel="noopener">BCv2 releases</a> on GitHub (Watch &gt; Custom &gt; Releases), or use the <a href="{{ bcv2Releases.feed }}">feed</a>.</p>
    </section>

    <p class="bcv2-signoff">Have fun with it, and tag me when you make use of it for something cool! :)</p>
  {% endif %}
  </div>
</article>
<script>
  // Links like /bcv2/#privacy point inside a closed section: open it, and
  // the section around it for #serial, first.
  (() => {
    const reveal = () => {
      const id = decodeURIComponent(location.hash.slice(1));
      const target = id && document.getElementById(id);
      let opened = false;
      for (let section = target && target.closest("details"); section; section = section.parentElement.closest("details")) {
        if (!section.open) section.open = opened = true;
      }
      if (opened) target.scrollIntoView();
    };
    reveal();
    addEventListener("hashchange", reveal);
  })();
</script>
